.TH XARGS 1 2026-10-20 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
xargs \- run a command with arguments read from input
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B xargs
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ command
.RI [ argument ...]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B xargs
reads items from standard input, or from a file with
.BR \-a ,
and runs
.I command
with the items added after its arguments.
With no
.IR command ,
it runs
.BR echo .
It runs
.I command
as many times as the items need, giving each run as many items as the
limits of
.B \-n
and
.B \-s
allow.
This
.B xargs
is a busybox applet; see
.BR busybox (1).
.PP
.I command
shares the standard input, output, and error of
.BR xargs .
When there are no items,
.I command
still runs once, with no added arguments, unless
.B \-r
is given.
.B xargs
writes nothing to standard output itself.
Errors are reported on standard error with an
.B xargs:
prefix.
.PP
.I command
is started as the kit's shell starts an external command: an applet of
that name runs inside busybox, and any other name without a directory
part is looked up in
.BR PATH ;
see
.B Running commands
in
.BR busybox (1),
and
.B NOTES
for a
.I command
with a directory part.
A file that begins with
.B #!\&
runs under the interpreter it names.
A shell built-in or function is not found.
.SS Input
Items are read as bytes.
By default they are separated by white space: a space, tab, newline,
vertical tab, form feed, or carriage return.
A run of separators counts as one, and leading and trailing separators
are ignored.
Quoting makes a separator part of an item, and the quoting characters
are removed:
.IP \(bu 2
A single or double quote starts a quoted string that ends at the next
quote of the same kind.
Inside it every other character is literal, including newline,
backslash, and the other kind of quote.
.IP \(bu 2
Outside quotes, a backslash makes the next character literal, even a
separator, a quote, or another backslash.
.IP \(bu 2
Quoted strings and unquoted text may follow one another within an item,
so
.B a\(aqb c\(aqd
is the single item
.BR "ab cd" .
A pair of quotes with nothing between them and nothing else around
them makes no item.
.IP \(bu 2
A quote still open at the end of the input is an error, except a quote
that is the last byte and starts an item, which is ignored.
On the error,
.B xargs
exits with status 1 without running
.I command
for the items read since its last run.
.PP
The options
.B \-0
and
.B \-I
read the input differently.
.SS Size limits
A run gets items until it has
.B \-n
of them, or until one more would take its size past the limit of
.BR \-s .
The size counts
.I command
and its arguments first, then the items, each as its length in bytes
plus 1 for a terminator.
When
.B xargs
starts the command as a new process, the command and its arguments
count as they appear on the Windows command line, quoted as in
.BR NOTES .
An item is never split between runs.
The default limit is 30720 bytes.
.PP
A command that
.B xargs
starts as a new process has a second limit, because Windows allows a
command line of about 32767 characters.
The items of a run, counted as they appear on the Windows command line,
with quotes and escapes and one space after each, total at most 32750
bytes.
When they would pass that,
.B xargs
runs those that fit and carries the rest into the next run, whatever
.B \-s
says, so that run may have more items or bytes than
.B \-n
or
.B \-s
allow.
The command and its own arguments are not counted, so a long command
can still make the command line too long:
.B xargs
then reports
.B Arg list too long
and exits with status 126.
An item that alone passes 32750 bytes ends the input without an error:
.B xargs
drops it and everything after it.
An applet that runs inside
.B xargs
has neither the quoting nor this limit; see
.BR NOTES .
.SH OPTIONS
Options may be grouped, as in
.BR \-rt .
An option's argument may be attached, as in
.BR \-n2 ,
or be the next argument, except for
.B \-e
and
.BR \-i ,
whose arguments must be attached.
The options end at the first argument that is not an option, which is
.IR command ,
or at
.BR \-\- .
Everything after belongs to the command, even arguments that begin with
.BR \- .
The only long option is
.BR \-\-no\-run\-if\-empty .
An invalid option, or an option with no argument it needs, prints an
error and the usage message on standard error and exits with status 1.
Numeric arguments are plain decimal numbers with no sign.
.TP
.B \-0
Read NUL-terminated items.
White space, quotes, and backslashes are not special, so an item may
contain them, and newlines.
The last item need not end with a NUL.
An empty item is passed as an empty argument.
.B \-E
has no effect.
.TP
.BI "\-a " file
Read items from
.I file
instead of standard input.
.I file
also becomes the standard input of the commands.
Since
.B xargs
reads ahead in blocks, they find it positioned after the last block
read, usually beyond the items used so far.
.B xargs
opens
.I file
before reading anything, and exits with status 1 if it cannot.
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-no\-run\-if\-empty
Do not run
.I command
when there are no items.
.B \-I
implies it.
.TP
.B \-t
Print each command line on standard error before running it: the
command and its arguments, separated by single spaces, then a newline.
Nothing is quoted.
.TP
.B \-p
Ask before each run.
.B xargs
prints the command line on standard error as
.B \-t
does but with no newline, then a space and
.BR ?...\& ,
and reads the answer from the console, not from standard input.
The keys typed are echoed up to Enter.
The command runs if the first key was
.B y
or
.BR Y .
Any other answer, including an empty one, skips that run, and
.B xargs
goes on to the next.
.TP
\fB\-E\fR \fIstr\fR, \fB\-e\fR[\fIstr\fR]
Set
.I str
as the logical end of the input.
When an item equals
.IR str ,
after its quotes are removed,
.B xargs
reads and discards the rest of the input, and runs
.I command
for the items before it.
Both options set the same string, and the last one given wins.
An empty
.I str
given to
.B \-E
means there is none, which is the default.
.B \-e
with no
.I str
changes nothing.
Neither has any effect with
.B \-0
or
.BR \-I .
.TP
\fB\-I\fR \fIstr\fR, \fB\-i\fR[\fIstr\fR]
Run
.I command
once for each input line, with every occurrence of
.I str
in
.I command
and in each of its arguments replaced by the line.
An argument without
.I str
is passed unchanged, and no other items are added.
Both options set the same string, and the last one given wins.
The default is
.BR {} ,
and
.B \-i
with no
.I str
changes nothing.
An empty
.I str
crashes
.BR xargs .
.IP
A line ends at a newline, or at a NUL with
.BR \-0 ,
or at the end of the input.
Leading white space is skipped.
Trailing white space is kept, including the carriage return of a CRLF
line end.
Lines that are empty or only white space are ignored.
Quotes and backslashes are not special.
A line that does not fit in the size limit is an error.
.B \-n
and
.B \-E
have no effect, and
.B \-r
is implied.
.TP
.BI "\-n " count
Pass at most
.I count
items to each run.
It is from 1 to 2147483647.
There is no limit by default but the size.
.TP
.BI "\-s " size
Limit each run to
.I size
bytes, as described under
.BR "Size limits" ,
instead of 30720.
It is from 1 to 2147483647, and
.B xargs
allocates a buffer of that size.
If
.I command
and its arguments leave no room for an item,
.B xargs
exits with status 1.
So does an item that cannot fit on its own.
.TP
.BI "\-P " procs
Run up to
.I procs
commands at the same time.
It is from 0 to 2147483647.
The default is 1, which waits for each command before starting the
next, and 0 means 64.
.B xargs
waits for the running commands before it exits.
See
.B NOTES
for what this changes.
.TP
.B \-x
Accepted and ignored.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a usage message on standard output and exit with status 0.
It is recognized only as the first argument.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B BB_GLOBBING
Unless it is
.BR 0 ,
wildcards in the arguments of
.B xargs
are expanded when it starts, as needed when run from
.BR cmd.exe .
That includes the arguments of
.IR command .
A busybox process started with it unset sets it to
.B 0
for its children, so
.I command
does not expand them again.
.TP
.B BB_OVERRIDE_APPLETS
Applets that are not run when named as
.IR command ,
so that a program in
.B PATH
runs instead; see
.BR busybox (1).
It applies to the
.B echo
used when there is no
.IR command .
.TP
.B PATH
Where
.I command
is looked up when it is not an applet and has no directory part.
.TP
.BR LC_ALL ", " LC_CTYPE ", " LANG
If the first of these that is set has the value
.BR C ,
what
.B xargs
writes to a console, such as with
.B \-t
and
.BR \-p ,
is not converted, and the console interprets the bytes in its own code
page.
.PP
Variables that affect every busybox applet, such as
.B BB_TERMINAL_MODE
for console output, are described in
.BR busybox (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Every run of
.I command
exited with status 0, or there was nothing to run.
.TP
.B 1
.B xargs
itself failed: an invalid option or number, an unmatched quote, an item,
or the command and its arguments, too long for the size limit, a
.B \-a
file that cannot be opened, or no memory.
.TP
.B 123
A run exited with a status from 1 to 254.
.B xargs
goes on with the rest of the input and exits with 123 at the end.
.TP
.B 124
A run exited with status 255, and
.B xargs
stopped without reading more input.
.TP
.B 125
A run was ended by a signal, and
.B xargs
stopped.
.TP
.B 126
.I command
was found but could not be run, such as when its command line is too
long, and
.B xargs
stopped.
.TP
.B 127
.I command
was not found, and
.B xargs
stopped.
.PP
Errors are reported on standard error as
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
xargs: unmatched single quote
xargs: unmatched double quote
xargs: argument line too long
xargs: can\(aqt fit single argument within argument list size limit
xargs: can\(aqt open \(aq\fIfile\fR\(aq: \fIreason\fR
xargs: invalid number \(aq\fIarg\fR\(aq
xargs: number \fIarg\fR is not in \fImin\fR..2147483647 range
xargs: \fIcommand\fR: \fIreason\fR
xargs: \fIcommand\fR: exited with status 255; aborting
xargs: \(aq\fIcommand\fR\(aq terminated by signal \fIn\fR
.EE
.RE
.SH EXAMPLES
List the C files below the current directory that mention
.BR main ,
whatever their names:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ find . \-name \(aq*.c\(aq \-print0 | xargs \-0 grep \-l main
.EE
.RE
.PP
Remove the backup files, if there are any:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ find . \-name \(aq*.bak\(aq \-print0 | xargs \-0 \-r rm
.EE
.RE
.PP
Show how the items are split, with two to a run.
Each command line is printed on standard error before it runs:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ printf \(aqa b c d e\en\(aq | xargs \-t \-n2 echo
echo a b
a b
echo c d
c d
echo e
e
.EE
.RE
.PP
Copy each file in a list.
Lines are items, so names with spaces survive:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ ls *.txt | xargs \-I{} cp {} backup/{}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Stop at a marker:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ printf \(aqa b END c\en\(aq | xargs \-E END echo
a b
.EE
.RE
.PP
Checksum every file with four processes at a time, eight files each:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ find . \-type f \-print0 | xargs \-0 \-n8 \-P4 sha256sum
.EE
.RE
.PP
Pass a Windows path.
The backslashes need quoting, or they are taken as escapes:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ echo \(aqC:\edir\esub\(aq | xargs echo
C:dirsub
$ echo \(aq"C:\edir\esub"\(aq | xargs echo
C:\edir\esub
.EE
.RE
.PP
Read the items from a file:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ xargs \-a files.txt wc \-l
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
Differences from GNU
.BR xargs :
.IP \(bu 2
There are no options beyond those above, and the only long option is
.BR \-\-no\-run\-if\-empty .
In particular there is no
.BR \-d ,
.BR \-L ,
.BR \-l ,
or
.BR \-o .
.IP \(bu 2
.B \-x
does nothing.
.IP \(bu 2
.B \-p
reads single keys from the console, and only the first key counts,
even if it is erased.
.IP \(bu 2
A quoted string may span lines, and an empty one makes no item.
.IP \(bu 2
.B \-I
does not process quotes or backslashes, and ignores
.B \-n
and
.BR \-E .
.IP \(bu 2
Without
.BR \-a ,
the commands inherit the standard input of
.B xargs
instead of an empty one.
With
.BR \-a ,
they get the file instead of the standard input of
.BR xargs .
.PP
A backslash in the input is an escape, so a Windows path loses its
backslashes:
.B C:\edir\esub
arrives as
.BR C:dirsub .
Quote such a path, double its backslashes, or use forward slashes.
The options
.B \-0
and
.B \-I
treat backslashes as ordinary characters.
.PP
Input is read in binary mode.
A carriage return is white space, so CRLF line ends do no harm to the
default mode.
With
.B \-I
the carriage return stays at the end of the line.
.PP
.I command
may be given with forward slashes or backslashes, and with a drive
letter.
A
.I command
with a directory part is not looked up in
.BR PATH ,
unless the directory is
.IR /bin ,
.IR /usr/bin ,
.IR /sbin ,
or
.IR /usr/sbin :
then the applet of that name runs if there is one, or else that file if
it exists, or else the program of that name in
.BR PATH .
The suffix
.BR .com ,
.BR .exe ,
.BR .sh ,
.BR .bat ,
or
.B .cmd
may be left off.
.PP
When
.B xargs
runs one command at a time, without
.B \-P
or with
.BR "\-P 1" ,
a command that is a NOFORK applet, such as
.BR echo ,
runs inside
.B xargs
itself;
.B busybox \-\-list\-full
marks these applets.
It gets its arguments as they are, so neither the quoting nor the
32750-byte limit of
.B Size limits
applies to it.
Any other command is started as a new process, and so is every command
with
.B \-P
0 or above 1.
Each argument is quoted for the Windows command line when it is passed.
.PP
A command's exit code is a 32-bit Windows value, which
.B xargs
maps as busybox
.B sh
does.
The code 0xC0000005 counts as signal 11, 0xC000013A as signal 2, and a
code whose top byte is a signal number and whose other bytes are zero,
as busybox programs use for signals, such as 0x0F000000 for signal 15,
as that signal.
Each gives status 125.
Any other code above 255 is reported on standard error as the Windows
message for it, if there is one, followed by
.B Error
and the code in hexadecimal with a
.B 0x
prefix, and
.B xargs
uses its low byte, or 255 if that is zero.
A command that exits with 256 stops
.B xargs
with status 124, and one that exits with 258 gives 123.
.PP
On Ctrl+C or Ctrl+Break,
.B xargs
sends
.B TERM
to each running command and its child processes, and exits at once with
the Windows exit code 0x02000000, which busybox
.B sh
reports as status 130.
.PP
With
.B \-P
0 or above 1, commands run as separate processes, and their output may
be interleaved.
.B xargs
looks at the exit code of a command only when it needs its slot, or at
the end, so after a failure it may start one more command before it
stops.
While 64 or more commands are running, which takes
.B \-P
0 or at least 64,
.B xargs
waits for one to end in a busy loop that keeps a processor busy.
It uses the Windows exit code as it is, without the mapping above: a
code from 256 to 0x17F counts as a failure, with status 123, a code of
0x180 or more is reported as a signal, with status 125, and a code of
0x80000000 or more as a command that could not be run, with status 126
or 127.
It also does not detect a command that cannot be started, such as one
that does not exist: it reports no error and waits forever.
Check that the command exists first.
.PP
The standard input of
.I command
is that of
.BR xargs ,
or the file of
.BR \-a ,
positioned after what
.B xargs
has read ahead.
There is no
.B \-o
to give it the keyboard instead.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR busybox (1),
.BR echo (1),
.BR find (1),
.BR sh (1)
